## The idea

Walk both trees at the same positions. Both present → a **new** node holding the sum. Only one present → a **copy** of that subtree. Building new nodes keeps both inputs unchanged.

## Step by step

1. Both null → null.
2. One null → copy the other.
3. Both present → new node (a + b), then merge the left children and the right children.

## Worked example

**Input:** a = [1,3,2,5], b = [2,1,3,null,4,null,7]

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| roots | 1 + 2 = 3 |
| left | 3 + 1 = 4; its children 5 (only a) and 4 (only b) |
| right | 2 + 3 = 5; right child 7 (only b) |

**Result:** [3, 4, 5, 5, 4, null, 7]

## Cost

O(n + m) time and memory.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// TreeNode is provided by the starter (Models.cs): int Value; TreeNode Left, Right.

// LeetCode 617 — overlay two trees: where both have a node, add the values; otherwise take whichever exists.
// Builds a NEW tree, so neither input changes (a caller may still be using them).
public static class MergeTrees {
    public static TreeNode Merge(TreeNode a, TreeNode b) {
        if (a == null && b == null) return null;
        if (a == null) return Copy(b);
        if (b == null) return Copy(a);
        var node = new TreeNode(a.Value + b.Value);
        node.Left = Merge(a.Left, b.Left);
        node.Right = Merge(a.Right, b.Right);
        return node;
    }

    private static TreeNode Copy(TreeNode t) {
        if (t == null) return null;
        var node = new TreeNode(t.Value);
        node.Left = Copy(t.Left);
        node.Right = Copy(t.Right);
        return node;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Mutate or copy?**

Ask. Mutating a is O(overlap) and allocation-free; copying is safe for callers who keep the inputs.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(n + m) time and memory for the copy.

**Q: Shared state?**

Read-only inputs — safe to merge concurrently from several threads.

**Q: Very deep trees.**

An explicit stack of node triples (a, b, out).

